<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Start here</h1>
  <%# #628. This account signed up to connect an assistant, confirmed its address somewhere the
      consent screen could not follow -- a phone, a mail app's browser -- and has not connected
      it since. Said first, because it is what they came for, and it says what will happen:
      pressing Connect again asks them to sign in, and since #628 that sign-in comes back to
      the consent screen rather than here. Gone once the connection exists. %>
  <%- if @connecting %>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-sky-50 p-4 text-sm text-sky-900 ring-1 ring-sky-200" role="status">
      You were connecting <%= @connecting %>. Go back to where you started and press Connect
      again. If it asks you to sign in, do, and you will come straight back to approve it.
    </p>
  <%- end %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    Tectonic Plates is a barbell log. It keeps what you lifted, works out what to load on
    the bar, and charts whether the weight is going up.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600">
    What makes it more than a list is that a session is written before it is trained: plan
    the week, then work off a screen at the rack that ticks each set off as you do it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Two ways in</h2>
  <%# The assistant first, because it is what /welcome promised -- "Ask your AI what to lift",
      #255 -- and since #411 it is also the only way a block gets written: there is no block
      editor any more, so the card that used to offer one led to an empty workouts list (#629).

      This used to argue the other way, that an assistant "is worth little until there is
      training for it to read". That was never quite true and is not now: a first block needs
      nothing but what the lifter says in conversation, a top weight rather than a history,
      which is the welcome page's own example of somebody coming back after six weeks off.
      The example sentence is there because a blank chat box is the second place a new lifter
      stops, after the connect button.

      Logging stays, second, for somebody with a session already behind them or no assistant
      at all -- it works on its own and gives the calendar something to show. %>
  <ol class="mt-3 space-y-4">
    <li class="rounded-lg bg-white p-4 shadow ring-1 ring-gray-200">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Plan your first block with your assistant</h3>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600">
        Connect your AI assistant to this account once, then tell it what you are training for.
        It writes the weeks, the days and the lifts, and the sessions appear here on the days
        you train, warmups and plate math included.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 rounded-md bg-gray-50 p-3 text-sm text-gray-700 ring-1 ring-gray-200">
        &ldquo;Write me a four-week block, three days a week, built around squat, bench and
        deadlift. I am coming back after six weeks off.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <%# Full width on a phone and only as wide as its words from 640px up: a thumb
          reaching across a phone screen wants the whole line, and a mouse does not. %>
      <a href="/connections"
         class="<%= button_style %> mt-3 inline-flex min-h-11 w-full items-center justify-center bg-lime-500 px-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600 sm:w-auto">Connect your assistant</a>
    </li>

    <li class="rounded-lg bg-white p-4 shadow ring-1 ring-gray-200">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Or log a session you have already done</h3>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600">
        Put in a date and the lifts you did, weight by weight. One session is enough for the
        calendar and the charts to have something to show.
      </p>
      <a href="/workouts/new"
         class="mt-3 inline-flex min-h-11 w-full items-center justify-center rounded-md bg-white px-4 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-700 shadow-sm ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-50 sm:w-auto">Log a workout</a>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-gray-500">
    Everything you log shows up on
    <a href="/" class="font-medium text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">the calendar</a>,
    which is the home page from here on.
  </p>
</div>
